8|null@AirForce^1438652739#3fp1dp:marclies$:As stupid as it sounds, I have questions about marriage...
1|0@AirForce^1438793690#ctshgv0:arky61$:Probably will get downvoted, but oh well. I am an A1C and married. Between dating and marriage I've been with my wife for about 5 years, and am 100% happy with my life. You will have a million salty techs tell you about there failed marriage. To which I listen, and then pretty much stop caring. I can tell you that being married is a lot harder than most people expect, especially the first year. If you are planning on getting married, make sure that you are both mature and responsible enough to handle being away from each other for 3-6 months and trust them not to cheat. Am currently on my second deployment and I have absolute faith that my wife is still loyal to me, and I would not be nearly as happy or even be the same person if I hadn't had time to live and grow with her. Tl;dr: Regardless of what the AF thinks, you are an adult, so do what you think best. Make sure you both are mature and ready to settle down though. Edit: also, go to a councillor before marriage, those guys will bring up things that you need to listen to. Give it 3 sessions or so and trust their word. It is their job to tell help you. 
1|0@AirForce^1438731418#ctrplfg:tehSQUEE$:If you do get married, keep your checking accounts seperate. Join the rest if you really must, but keep your own personal checking and savings account. My spouse used her PoA to move all my money around into different accounts and spent it all. I had absolutely no reason to expect her to do it, but she did. We were happy, but money is tempting. 
2|0@AirForce^1438685140#ctqygzu:starsin$:I'm an A1C myself, with an Air National Guard unit (which seems to deploy a lot...), so I can relate in some ways, but not all. If memory serves me correctly, I've got a buddy from BMT stationed out there at Travis as well and can try to reach out to them as well. I also spent time out in Cali near Travis (if you get the chance, go down to Vacaville for some shopping at the outlet malls down there...killer prices on stuff). Anyways, introductions and stuff aside....Marriage. I'm married, got married literally about 2 and a half weeks before I left for BMT, and was very fortunate to not have a long Tech School. But it was rough, for both of us. However, in the end, we both feel like it did help our relationship because at some point, I will deploy again for longer than a couple of weeks for a TDY...an actual deployment. I digress (apologies...long day, my brain wanders when I get tired) - if you two are compatible, and are truly committed to each other, make the jump. I know...controversial advice right there. But being married is pretty much one of the greatest things in the world: being able to share yourself (no...not just physically, though sex is an important part to any marriage) completely and totally with someone else that you trust and love is something that I find difficulty to put words to. Knowing that they'll have your back at all times, unconditionally...knowing that I have someone that I can share my everything with - my fears, hopes, dreams, vulnerabilities, stresses, loves (like, you know..."I love cake"), desires, etc - is just darn near the best thing I've got going for me. Yes, you make more money as a married person in the military (something I didn't know until I told my MSgt that I was married right before leaving for BMT), and as Active Duty, you can move out of the dorms into married housing and all that cool jazz. I suppose what it comes down to, is my advice would be do it for the right reasons. Do it because you love each other, because you trust each other with one another's hearts (cheesy, I know, but some will understand, some won't and I'm not worried about those that won't). Yes, you will find a lot of people who will say don't do it, it only leads to heartbreak. And I will freely admit that it can: marriage is a gamble where from the very beginning you're going 100% all in. But when it pays off, after all the struggles and everything that life throws at you...it is completely worth it all. tl;dr - do it for the right reasons: love and trust. If you feel it's right thing to do for you and your boyfriend...do it and don't let anybody dissuade you from doing it. edit: expecting a lot of salt...but, it's my opinion and my stance, so....yeah. I'm okay with that. We're all free to express our own opinions. Thus, I hope that my advice isn't taken as "do this!"
1|0@AirForce^1438682601#ctqxupf:militisdominus$:Don't get married.
1|0@AirForce^1438686931#ctqyxz2:thedaywemet$:What will getting married do for you? If bf enlists, there is no guarantee he'll be stationed with you.
1|0@AirForce^1438700908#ctr55is:FoxFourTwo$:Assuming your boyfriend isn't in the same location as you, I would highly recommend staying in the dorms and banking. My boyfriend and I were seperated for a long period of time; he was deployed to Iraq and later on Afghanistan so I saw no reason for us to get married at the time. I stayed in the dorms, saved and saved. When I had enough saved, I saved even more. He returned from his deployment and we ended up getting a place together after it was my time to leave the dorms. We didnt get married until three years after that. And we had been together for seven years before that. My point is, plan wisely. Don't make sudden and permanent decisions on a temporary issue. Give yourself plenty of time to get to know each other on the deepest levels before even considering moving in with someone. If you can't stand their idiosyncrasies, the relationship won't last long. Save money. :P 
31|0@AirForce^1438655594#ctqnzr0:riotupfront$:This is going to sound negative and salty as fuck, and you probably won't listen anyways, here's my take on marriage as a young airman. Just wait. I know the BAH/BAS looks tempting. I know everyone else is doing it. I know room inspections suck. And I know single airman are treated as second class in the military. But just wait. I was a single airman stationed at Travis my entire enlistment, and the first three years sucked (this was back when the dorms were dry). As much as I hated it, not getting married was one of the best decisions I ever made. Now while the rest of my friends have multiple divorces under their belts, are still paying off loans from their 20,000$ marriages, and are living like 40 year olds in their early 20's, I'm able to just kick back, save money, and enjoy life. I've gotten to go on vacations 4-5 times a year, drive a nice car, have a solid group of friends, plenty of exes (and valuable relationship experience from them), and plenty of money invested away for the future. When I do meet someone I want to spend my life with, I'll be set up so that I'll be financially ready and will have enjoyed my 20's to the extent that I'm ready to move onto that next stage in life. Most importantly though, everyone changes big time over the course of their enlistment. I guarantee in a couple of years you and your boyfriend will be almost completely different people and who knows if you'll even be compatible. Just wait, and if you guys are still together after you've found some balance in your life/career THEN go for it. I'm guessing you guys haven't even lived together yet, and trust me that's when you really get to know someone. If you got married tomorrow, what happens when he joins and has to do a year long tech school? What if he graduates and moves out to you, only to find out that you deploy in month? Would you guys be able to last? These are just a couple of many scenarios you could potentially deal with that happen all the time, and that's why practically EVERY young airman marriage falls apart in less than a year. Just give it time, wait a few years and if you guys are still going strong then consider it. But chances are you're going to do the exact opposite of what I said, stress out your supervisor to the point of him/her becoming an alcoholic, and fall into one of the worst case scenarios that I mentioned above. Whatever happens, good luck.
