2|0@AirForce^1438664189#ctqsbwy:EveryDayImJavelin$:You definitely notice the reduction if you max out your contributions. I think I put 20% in and that's a significant chunk down the road. But the advantage, if I remember correctly, is you get taxed now rather than later. Which is a plus, if you're of the "taxes are only going to proportionately rise" school of thought. I did my research two years ago and just decided to not touch my TSP, so I've forgotten some of the details. 
1|0@AirForce^1438696010#ctr2ee4:ciderczar$:That and taxes are lower now than they've been since Eisenhower. Take advantage of that fact.
1|0@AirForce^1438696158#ctr2h0z:police-disco-lights$:Exactly. And it's smart for military members to do a Roth TSP since a lot of our money (BAH, BAS) isn't taxable. So glad they added the Roth option a few years ago.
